    "method": "Two-stage geodetic inversion of coseismic offsets from 62 GNSS sites. The first stage constrained fault geometry with uniform slip using simulated annealing followed by BFGS optimization; second stage used a steepest-descent constrained optimization on a distributed-slip fault and Okada elastic half-space Green functions. Figure-digitized colored distributed-slip panel resampled to 11 x 12 cells.",
    "green_function_model": "Okada (1985) rectangular dislocation in homogeneous elastic half-space.",
    "slip_type": "variable",
    "smoothing_constraint": false,
    "notes": "FIGURE-DIGITIZED, NOT AUTHOR ORIGINAL PATCH VALUES. The source image is the colored \"Slip Distribution\" panel on the PDF page displaying printed journal page 733 (PDF page 18), adjacent to the erroneously/misleadingly placed Figure 10 caption about coseismic displacement. The paper section 3.3 describes a 43 x 30 km model with nominal 2.5 x 4 km elements, strike 288, dip 46 and rake -84 degrees (focal model Table 2). The raster extracted directly from the PDF is an unoccluded 11-column x 13-color-band graphic; it is not a provided machine-readable inversion patch array. Resample its 13 displayed vertical color bands using area-weighted averages onto a 11 x 12 patch mesh, compatible with 30/2.5 km down dip and approximately 43/4 km along strike. Resulting along-strike patch length is 43/11 = 3.909 km, rather than exactly 4 km. Color values were calibrated to the embedded colorbar, assumed to run 0-1.8 m (tick labels extend to 1.6 m); small legend-background values below 0.035 m were set to zero. Uncertainty is at least several hundredths of a meter, higher for resampled colors and near the extremum. The figure horizontal axis is longitude (26.88-27.02 E), whose range is much shorter than the physical along-strike length of about 43 km: it must NOT be used to derive patch geographic coordinates. The figure vertical axis labels depth in km (0-30 km), whereas the fault-grid down-dip distance combined with the 46-degree dip would produce shallower vertical depths. This inconsistency/axis ambiguity cannot be resolved from the displayed 2-D figure. The stored depth_km values follow a geometric planar-plane convention from the top edge (down_dip_km * sin(46 deg)); they are NOT direct readings of the printed 0-30-km vertical axis. Patch latitudes and longitudes are intentionally null. The Table 2 coordinates 37.809 N, 26.901 E are listed for the model reference/midpoint in the paper, but are not demonstrably the fault-plane upper left endpoint and therefore are not used to anchor this local grid. The earthquake location uses the source-listed seismic catalog entry in Table 1, not this geodetic plane reference. Slip below 12.5 km and slip >1m above 7.5 km are published results, but their direct depth interpretation in this reconstructed grid is limited by the figure coordinate ambiguity. All reconstructed patches use the published nominal rake -84 degrees; patch-specific rake values were not recovered. Values should not be treated as an original inversion data product."
  },
